  {
    "alert_id": "EX3_ALERT_002",
    "title": "Dyslipidemia with High LDL",
    "message": "The patient has dyslipidemia with an LDL cholesterol of 4.2 mmol/L and total cholesterol of 6.4 mmol/L. Guidelines recommend screening for cardiovascular risk factors. The patient's BMI of 33 and age of 45 are additional risk factors. The clinician's plan does not address this finding. While ART can affect lipids, untreated hypothyroidism is also a common cause of secondary dyslipidemia.",
    "issue_type": "monitoring_gap",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "Address dyslipidemia per cardiovascular risk management guidelines, considering both ART and hypothyroidism.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "EX3_ALERT_003",
    "title": "Potential Misattribution of Weight Gain",
    "message": "The clinician's plan attributes weight gain solely to DTG and considers switching. Guidelines note that the association between DTG and weight gain may not be causal but a result of less metabolic toxicity compared to older regimens. The patient has significant weight gain (12 kg over 3 years, BMI 33) and clear signs of uncontrolled hypothyroidism, which is a potent cause of weight gain. A switch may be premature without first optimizing thyroid treatment.",
    "issue_type": "guideline_deviation",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "Optimize treatment for hypothyroidism and reassess weight trend before considering an ART switch for weight gain.",
